Why I chose those two perspectives

I didn’t choose them because “humans think like this, AIs think like that” as fixed essences.

I used a receiver-affordance model:

\boxed{
\text{Reading Perspective}
=
f(
\text{reader architecture},
\text{available memory},
\text{salient handles},
\text{goal},
\text{representation}
)
}

For the AI lens, I weighted explicit schemas, definitions, relationships, examples, executable transformations, dependency structure, reconstruction rules, provenance and tests. Transformer-family systems operate over contextual representations through attention, and in-context learning research demonstrates that supplied context can materially alter computation/task behavior without changing the underlying trained parameters. 

For the human lens, I weighted chronology, people/roles, motive, surprise, conflict, recurring motifs, humor, emotional salience, developmental change and retrospective reinterpretation because those give a human reader a coherent situation model rather than merely an operator graph. The human narrative literature supports the importance of those evolving situation dimensions in comprehension. 

But swap the reader and the weighting can swap too. A software engineer may read Pantheon more like my “AI” interpretation. An AI asked to reconstruct its intellectual history may produce something much closer to the human story.

So reader type is another ruler to Hammer.

